From bc5c8e747808b5b4bfa408798b591b70a59d7ab5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 07:33:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Judge applying from the sign of the orb's rate
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

findAspects moved both bodies 0.02 day along their speeds and called an
aspect applying when the moved orb was smaller. For a pair closing at orb
rate r that is false whenever the orb is under 0.01 r, so every aspect read
as separating for the last 14.4 minutes before exact (audit ledger
angles-houses-aspects-2). The documented contract was already
instantaneous.

aspectMotion now takes the signed separation, the deviation from the
aspect's angle and the relative speed. It calls the aspect applying when the
orb is strictly decreasing, separating when it is exact or growing, and
stationary when the relative speed is below 1e-9°/day or is not a number.
Aspect.applying keeps its boolean. aspectMotion, AspectMotion and
STATIONARY_RELATIVE_SPEED are exported from the root and the internal math
entry.

Tests: named cases, including a retrograde pair and the wrapped-separation
case that the brief's shorthand gets wrong. A seeded sweep of 400,000
pairs against linear motion finds no errors; the old step misjudges 769 of
them. The engine's own positions every 30 minutes through the first quarter
of 2024, judged by the orb's actual motion over ±1 s, also show no errors.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
